<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kuala Lumpur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1857</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kuala Lumpur grew from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>-mining settlement founded around 1857 at the meeting of the Klang and Gombak rivers in Selangor. Chinese miners and their leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yap-ah-loy">Yap Ah Loy</a> built it up, and in 1880 it became the capital of Selangor. It was the seat of the Federated Malay States from 1896 and is now the capital of Malaysia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kuala-lumpur.jpg" alt="Kuala Lumpur" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Renek78 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bukit_Bintang_junction_in_2024_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…state normalised relations with Indonesia in its first year, and Indonesia came to back its entry into ASEAN: applied for in 2011, accepted in principle in November 2022 and, as announced on 27 May 2025, completed at the October 2025 summit in <b>Kuala Lumpur</b> as the 11th member.</blockquote><a href="/empire/east-timor">East Timor · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Malaysia is a federal constitutional monarchy of thirteen states and three federal territories: <b>Kuala Lumpur</b>, Labuan and Putrajaya, the administrative centre to which ministries moved from 1999.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Indonesia answered with Konfrontasi, whose saboteurs bombed MacDonald House in March 1965, and quarrels with <b>Kuala Lumpur</b> over trade and race brought the 1964 riots.</blockquote><a href="/empire/singapore">Singapore · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1895 these were combined into the Federated Malay States, under a Resident-General in <b>Kuala Lumpur</b>, the first being Frank Swettenham, to unify administration, finance and railways.</blockquote><a href="/state/federated-malay-states">Federated Malay States</a></li><li><blockquote>The union was ruled by a British governor, Sir Edward Gent, from <b>Kuala Lumpur</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/malayan-union">Malayan Union</a></li><li><blockquote>Chinese miners poured in during the 19th century, and the rivalry of chiefs and secret societies erupted in the Klang War of 1867 to 1874, in which Yap Ah Loy built up <b>Kuala Lumpur</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/selangor-sultanate">Selangor Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
